The regex character class `[-\u2012\u2013\u2014\u2015\u2212]` includes a hyphen `-` at the beginning. In a regex character class, `-` has special meaning (range) unless it's at the beginning or end! So `[-\u2012\u2013\u2014\u2015\u2212]` is actually a range from `-` (hyphen, ASCII 45) to `\u2012` (figure dash, Unicode 8210)! That's a HUGE range of characters!

Actually, in Python regex, `-` in a character class is only treated as a range operator if it's between two characters. At the beginning of the class, it's just a literal `-`. So `[-\u2012\u2013\u2014\u2015\u2212]` means: match `-`, `\u2012`, `\u2013`, `\u2014`, `\u2015`, or `\u2212`. That's correct!

But wait, actually `-` at the beginning of a character class IS safe. The problem is when `-` is in the middle, like `[a-z]`. So `[-\u2012\u2013\u2014\u2015\u2212]` is fine.

